# Line endings.
#
# Without this, the repo had no policy: this machine has core.autocrlf=true
# (commits CRLF->LF, checks out LF->CRLF) while the Linux boxes have it unset.
# The same file could therefore be committed LF from one machine and CRLF from
# another, and git would report every single line as changed - which is exactly
# how lisp/joe-tools.el ended up as one whole-file merge conflict rather than
# two edits in different parts of the file.
#
# LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree on all platforms.
# Emacs reads LF perfectly well on Windows, so there is no reason to keep two
# conventions alive.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows script hosts are less forgiving than Emacs - cmd.exe and Windows
# Script Host can choke on LF-only files. None of these are tracked today
# (emacs-daemon.vbs and start-daemon.cmd are currently untracked), but setting
# it now means it is already correct if they are ever added.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.vbs  text eol=crlf

# Never apply text conversion to these.
*.elc  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary
